#!/usr/bin/env bash
# For someone building a system on top of coding agents, deciding whether to hold a
# process per session or spawn one per turn.
#
# Four facts a pool design actually stands on, measured rather than argued:
#
#   1. The concurrency distribution -- how many sessions are mid-turn at once, versus how
#      many hold a process. This is what sizes the pool, and the gap is usually large.
#   2. Per-session resident cost in PSS, which is what capacity arithmetic needs.
#   3. Whether a prompt-cache entry survives the process that wrote it. If it does not,
#      turn-scoping has a per-turn token cost. (It does. Measured token-exactly.)
#   4. What a turn actually pays to start, and where those seconds really go.
#
# SPENDS MODEL REQUESTS: roughly 8-10 cheap ones. Set SKIP_PAID=1 to run only the free
# parts.
set -uo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" || exit 2
I=instruments

say() { printf '\n\033[1m=== %s ===\033[0m\n' "$1"; }

say "1. Active vs resident concurrency -- the number that sizes the pool"
cat <<'NOTE'
A resident-session model provisions for the peak and pays for the floor. Compare the mean
and p50 below against the number of session trees in step 2: if the active mean is a small
fraction of the resident count, the floor is what you are paying for.
Minutes with zero active sessions are counted in the denominator deliberately.
NOTE
python3 "$I/measure_active_concurrency.py" "$@" || true

say "2. Resident cost per session -- use the PSS column"
cat <<'NOTE'
Capacity arithmetic on summed RSS is pessimistic BY CONSTRUCTION, not conservatively:
the agent binary's pages are shared across sessions, so RSS counts them once per session.
Price pool workers at a MID-TURN peak, not at this idle resident figure -- a worker doing
work costs more than a session sitting open.
NOTE
python3 "$I/measure_session_footprint.py" "$@" || true

say "3. Work a turn boundary would kill -- the one class that vetoes pooling"
cat <<'NOTE'
A session owning a dev server or a watch build cannot be turn-scoped: the worker exits and
takes the work with it. This is the question whose answer can veto the architecture, so
read the exit code: 0 = real count, 4 = floor from the degraded method, 3 = do not believe it.
NOTE
python3 "$I/census_background_work.py" "$@" || true

if [ "${SKIP_PAID:-0}" = "1" ]; then
  say "Skipping the paid measurements (SKIP_PAID=1)"
  echo "Steps 4 and 5 spend model requests and were not run. That is not a result."
  exit 0
fi

say "4. Does a prompt-cache entry survive the process that wrote it?"
cat <<'NOTE'
This is the objection that looks most expensive on paper: that a resident process holds a
warm cache a fresh worker would lose. Arm 5 is the one that settles it -- a writer process
exits, then two new processes resume, one with the same prefix marker and one without.
Watch for the EXACT line. If arm 4's control FAILED on your run, arms 1-2 proved nothing
that run; arm 5 carries its own control and is unaffected.
NOTE
python3 "$I/bench_cache_residency.py" "$@" || true

say "5. What a turn pays to start, and where the seconds go"
cat <<'NOTE'
Two figures, because the cheap one is a floor: boot stops at the first stream event, where
tools are still connecting; a full turn to `result` includes one inference round-trip.
The gap between them is mostly INFERENCE, which varies by an order of magnitude run to
run -- not tool connect, which is tens of milliseconds. Do not attribute the gap to MCP.
NOTE
REPS="${REPS:-3}" python3 "$I/bench_cold_start.py" || true
python3 "$I/bench_mcp_connect.py" "$@" || true

say "Done"
cat <<'NOTE'
Read docs/limits.md before quoting any of this. In particular: these are readings from one
box on one day, the caching result is n=1 per arm, and the instruments report spreads
because twice in this work the spread WAS the finding.
NOTE
